WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.897 --> 00:00:07.480
DYANN F WIRTH: My name is Dyann Wirth.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.470
And I'm professor of immunology and infectious disease at the Harvard TH

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.810
Chan School of Public Health.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:21.425
I'm here today interviewing Dr. Kent Kester on his work in malaria.

00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:23.690
Kent, please introduce yourself.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.920
KENT KESTER: I'm Kent Kester.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.830
I'm a professor of medicine at Unifored Services University

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.910
and head of translational science and biomarkers at Sanonfi Pasteur.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.260
DYANN F WIRTH: Can you tell me how you first got involved to work on malaria?

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.830
What were your first experiences?

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.830
KENT KESTER: It was kind of an accidental thing.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.820
I was an Infectious Disease Fellow in the early 1990s

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.680
at Walter Reed Army Medical Center and ended up

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.050
working doing my research rotation as an Infectious Disease Fellow

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:55.190
with people who I knew in the Army who ended up working on malaria.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:56.360
One thing led to another.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.940
And research project ended.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.020
My fellowship ended.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.740
I graduated, stayed on at Walter Reed and ended up

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.370
doing malaria vaccine development and more for probably

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.920
the next 20 years in the Army.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.200
DYANN F WIRTH: What do you think the biggest R&amp;amp;D

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.710
challenges are that we face today and that maybe we've

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.104
faced over the last decade?

00:01:17.104 --> 00:01:18.770
KENT KESTER: From my perspective, it's--

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.690
one of the biggest ones is funding.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.950
We recognize that developing effective interventions,

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:31.370
whether it's drugs or vaccines against malaria, requires sustained funding.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:36.162
And piecemeal funding, small grants, and awards just don't do it.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:38.120
And if you're going to develop something that's

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.870
going to have a global population-based impact,

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.120
it really has to be done at an industrial scale.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.320
So funding is number one.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.230
Number two is sustaining interest on the part of the scientific community.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:56.250
There's no shortage of research scientists who like to work on malaria.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.450
They have their favorite enzyme, their antigens.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.100
And it's all good stuff.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:07.250
But the challenge is, is that as sort of the behemoth of scientific funding

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:12.080
and areas of emphasis move along from innovative area to newer innovative

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.780
areas, sometimes interest gets lost. .

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.420
And so it's important to maintain that interest,

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.920
to grow next generation of researchers, and to keep the pressure on

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:22.990
to get things done.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.140
DYANN F WIRTH: So you've been in the military.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:25.460
And now you're in industry.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.250
And you've been exposed to the World Health Organization

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.620
and public-private partnerships.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.060
Can you give us a sense of the roles of these organizations, how they differ

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.685
and how they've changed over the years?

00:02:38.405 --> 00:02:41.480
KENT KESTER: You know, I think one of the big impacts of sort

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:48.190
of the worldwide malaria research, malaria R&amp;amp;D research, elimination,

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:53.150
eradication campaigns, has been this concept of public-private partnerships.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:57.440
It's clear that academic groups, while they have great ideas, great science,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.800
a lot of enthusiastic, motivated young researchers,

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.820
don't necessarily have the wherewithal to get products across the finish line.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.090
Industry, while it can do things at a big scale,

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.890
can get a lot of things made and deployed,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.150
have the regulatory throughput, don't always necessarily

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:19.570
have that spark of early-stage discovery interest.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:22.490
So that's a disconnect.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:26.760
Government agencies, nonprofits, I think they tend to fill in the gaps.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.040
And so it really is an important concept of these public-private partnerships.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.470
It's an evolving one.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:37.670
I think where we are now as to where we were, say, in the early '90s,

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.140
we're light years ahead.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:43.410
DYANN F WIRTH: In thinking about the development, say, of the RTS.S vaccine,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.900
which you had familiarity with as you were working in the military

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.700
and now in your role at Sanofi, looking at vaccines more generally,

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.580
what made that something to push forward?

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.510
As you say, it took a long time.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:01.700
The RTS,S vaccine was decades in development.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.160
But now it is an effective vaccine in humans.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:10.280
KENT KESTER: I think there was much work done with RTS,S and its predecessors

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.090
for a number of years that didn't really go anywhere-- a lot of failed trials,

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.730
small studies, five, 10, 15 volunteers.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.070
And it was really the advent of combining RTS,S, the antigen,

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:26.360
with novel adjuvants that had been developed at that time by SmithKline

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.350
Beecham, now GlaxoSmithKline, that really showed for the first time that

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.390
effective protection could be achieved.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.530
I think if that hadn't happened, the program probably would have died.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:40.670
That finding of significant protection, it really--

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:43.040
I don't want to say it put the program on steroids.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.530
But it certainly propelled it better and made

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.780
believers of people who previously had thought

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.310
that it wasn't going to go anywhere.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.060
DYANN F WIRTH: As you think to the future,

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.230
where do you see malaria vaccine development going?

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:59.450
And where do you see it kind of fitting into the overall vaccine development?

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.540
The world of vaccines has changed.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:05.022
And and where does malaria fit in this newer world?

00:05:05.022 --> 00:05:06.480
KENT KESTER: It's a great question.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.720
You know, if you look at RTS,S, in many ways, as you say,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.580
it's gone through really decades of work to get where we are now.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.190
It's kind of an old style vaccine.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.150
That's not necessarily a bad thing.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:22.620
But if we were starting de novo today, we probably

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.390
would do things a lot differently.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:31.080
We have much different insights into malaria immunology, protection

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.900
against disease, in some ways analogous to what's

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.780
happened on a larger scale in HIV.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:42.960
So the whole fight against HIV has just developed huge amounts of knowledge

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.550
about immunology.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:48.940
And I think the RTS,S work and allied efforts,

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:53.040
other groups who want to develop another vaccine or have different antigenic

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.630
idea, building on the work that the RTS,S program has done,

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:59.440
I think has really helped us a lot.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.145
We have a vaccine that clearly shows some degree of protection.

00:06:03.145 --> 00:06:04.020
DYANN F WIRTH: Right.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.978
KENT KESTER: As you say, it has its advantages.

00:06:05.978 --> 00:06:08.190
And it has its limitations.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:12.420
I think now we're at a point where we can take a step back, the field,

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.160
and say, OK, how can we make this better?

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.910
How can we leverage new technologies, whether it's

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:23.010
genomics or other types of omics things, new ways of interrogating

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:27.570
the immune response and really discerning correlates of protection,

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:28.510
et cetera.

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.690
So I think it's really been groundbreaking.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:33.000
The only thing that's been a bit discouraging,

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.000
of course, which goes back to my comment before about funding,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.530
it's taken a long time.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:42.510
So if you look in the industry, if you have an idea, let's say, for a quote,

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:48.810
unquote &amp;quot;routine vaccine,&amp;quot; whatever that means, from idea to approval

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.090
to deployment may take 15 to 20 years.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:55.320
It may cost up to $1 billion for all the trials, all the production,

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:56.379
all that kind of stuff.

00:06:56.379 --> 00:06:59.420
And if you look at the amount of funding that's gone to malaria vaccines,

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.490
while it's a lot of money, it's not $1 billion.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:03.370
DYANN F WIRTH: Yeah.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:05.640
It's a very interesting perspective.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.850
In thinking about vaccines in general, I mean, there's

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:11.130
this push now to get Zikka vaccine.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:16.540
And we hear that Zikka vaccines will be available really quickly.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.650
So in contrast to this long development period,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:23.070
what makes that technologically possible?

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:27.324
But also, what makes that possible kind of in an advocacy sense?

00:07:27.324 --> 00:07:30.240
KENT KESTER: The big difference is, of course, is malaria is parasite.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.470
So parasites have evolved with us for millennia.

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.020
And so they know how to hide out.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.060
They know how to live with us in most cases.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:45.100
There are multiple antigenic variations of parasites.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:46.650
So that's part of it.

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:48.780
Zikka, it's one strain.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:49.800
It's a virus.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.200
We know how to make viral vaccines.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.270
You know, it's fairly straightforward.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.160
And I'm not saying that that's necessarily a slam dunk.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.280
But it's much easier than developing a vaccine against a virus.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:03.750
There's already been a dengue vaccine that's

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:06.340
been licensed a number of countries around the world.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.754
There is yellow fever vaccine.

00:08:07.754 --> 00:08:10.170
And there are other vaccine candidates against other types

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:14.460
of flaviviruses that, in experimental settings, actually work very well.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.330
I think that the concept of getting a Zikka vaccine

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:22.830
technically made and along the approval pathway,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:24.640
I think that's not out of the question.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.440
I think the bigger challenge for Zikka is, how do you show efficacy

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.200
in a setting of perhaps an unstable epidemic, which we still

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.590
don't understand, when 80% of the people are actually asymptomatic?

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.534
So there's still some unknowns there.

00:08:37.534 --> 00:08:39.659
But I think from a technical standpoint, developing

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:41.717
a Zikka vaccine is much, much easier.

00:08:41.717 --> 00:08:44.800
DYANN F WIRTH: So in some ways, the vaccine like the yellow fever vaccine,

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.470
which was one of the first successful vaccines, led the way.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:52.980
In some ways, you can think about RTS,S maybe leading the way for malaria

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.330
vaccines in the same way.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:57.010
Because the parasite is much more complex.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.790
So I think that is a very interesting the analogy

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:03.291
to how to think about where we are in malaria vaccine development.

00:09:03.291 --> 00:09:04.290
KENT KESTER: Absolutely.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.690
You show some initial protection.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:08.610
You show initial value.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.970
And if there's a need, hopefully the funders, the advocacy

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.709
groups, and the scientists who have the good ideas will be able to run with it.

00:09:15.709 --> 00:09:17.250
And I think that's what we're seeing.

00:09:17.266 --> 00:09:19.140
DYANN F WIRTH: In terms of eradication, which

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.650
is the kind of focus of what we're talking about in general,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:28.080
do you think the vaccine needs to be there in order for eradication

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.550
to be successful?

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:33.360
You know, if I think about it, the diseases

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:37.740
that are eradicated are almost eradicated in the world

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.140
primarily are through the use of a vaccine.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:44.812
So do you think it's possible to eradicate malaria without a vaccine?

00:09:44.812 --> 00:09:46.770
And I'd be interested in your thoughts on that.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:48.520
KENT KESTER: It's an interesting question.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:52.050
You know, as a vaccine person and as a malaria vaccine person, of course,

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.720
I have to say yes, that it's important.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.340
But from a-- when one takes a step back, when we think about controlling,

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:04.200
eradicating, eliminating malaria, a lot of the approaches are low tech, right?

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.950
Get rid of mosquitoes.

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:07.690
Diagnose and treat people.

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:10.030
Get rid of standing water, these sorts of things.

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:13.180
However, again, these are parasites.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.040
They're very wily parasites.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.330
They're very persistent.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:20.020
If one is trying to develop sort of a comprehensive approach

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:23.650
to eliminate malaria, I think there's a role

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:28.570
for a vaccine just as much as there's a role for mosquito control and bed nets

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:29.887
and more.

00:10:29.887 --> 00:10:31.720
DYANN F WIRTH: What sort of advice would you

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:34.600
give someone who's kind of early in their career,

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:41.560
or even pre-career, on how to prepare themselves for a leadership role,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:43.220
as you've assumed in your field?

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.980
KENT KESTER: I think part of it is, is don't just limit

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:51.510
oneself to excellence at the bench.

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:52.510
That's really important.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:55.090
You have to be scientifically credible.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.520
However, if you really want to make a difference,

00:10:57.520 --> 00:10:59.110
you' got to understand the issues.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:03.340
You have to understand, how does a vaccine fit into a larger strategy?

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.930
How does that fit into low tech approaches and public policy and WHO

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.000
recommendations, et cetera, et cetera?

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.940
From a realistic standpoint, they really need

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.760
to be engaged just beyond the bench--

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:21.370
understand the issues, make contacts, and really be involved.

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.400
And it's not necessarily political advocacy.

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:30.232
It's scientific connectivity and being engaged and being visible.

00:11:30.232 --> 00:11:32.440
DYANN F WIRTH: That's a great way to think about it--

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:36.099
having them reach out to others that have complementary skills.

00:11:36.099 --> 00:11:36.890
KENT KESTER: Right.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:40.490
You and I have both dealt with many people in our careers

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:41.930
who have shown interest.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:44.152
And we've helped point them in those directions.

00:11:44.152 --> 00:11:46.360
And I think we can count a number of those people who

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:49.720
are now developing leadership roles of their own,

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:50.929
which is a really good thing.

00:11:50.929 --> 00:11:52.886
DYANN F WIRTH: Well, thank you very much, Kent.

00:11:52.886 --> 00:11:53.920
This has been fantastic.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:54.711
KENT KESTER: Great.

00:11:54.711 --> 00:11:55.860
Thanks.

